<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopian Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1974">1974</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ethiopian Civil War was fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> and anti-government rebels from 12 September 1974 to 28 May 1991. The Derg overthrew Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> and repressed nationalist fronts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">EPLF</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigray-peoples-liberation-front">TPLF</a> and the OLF with large counterinsurgency campaigns and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-red-terror">Qey Shibir</a> (Red Terror). The invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> in 1977 drew a Soviet and Cuban intervention, and the 1983–1985 famine and economic decline later strengthened the rebels. The war ended when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengistu-haile-mariam">Mengistu Haile Mariam</a> fled; at least 1.4 million people died.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 1950s discontent grew within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>'s regime. The government's repression and its ban on autonomous organisations stifled reform within the ruling class, and by the 1960s quality of life was stagnating and human rights abuses were widespread. A radical student movement called for land reform and democratisation. In December 1960 the Imperial Guard attempted a coup, which the army and air force put down; this made the armed forces a pillar of the regime. The army was later used against student protests, peasant uprisings and revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, and its morale fell as living conditions worsened. By 1973 many observers thought it held the real power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 12 September 1974 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>, a committee of low-ranking officers and enlisted men, overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>. On 21 March 1975 it abolished the monarchy and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, and Haile Selassie was killed in the National Palace on 27 August 1975. Most industries and urban real estate were nationalised. The Derg faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a>, which had fought since 1961, the Eritrean Peoples Liberation Front, the monarchist Ethiopian Democratic Union, the Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Party and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigray-peoples-liberation-front">Tigray People's Liberation Front</a>. In 1976 it began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-red-terror">Qey Shibir</a>, or Red Terror, against the Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Party and later the All-Ethiopia Socialist Movement. It escalated on 3 February 1977 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengistu-haile-mariam">Mengistu Haile Mariam</a> became chairman. Estimates of the victims run from 30,000 to 750,000.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> called up 'peasant marches', of whom 30,000 took part in the first assault. By June 1977 the Western Somali Liberation Front had forced the Ethiopian army out of much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a> into Jigjiga, Dire Dawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>. Somalia's invasion that year brought a Soviet and Cuban intervention that drove back its forces, and a Soviet-armed counteroffensive reversed the Eritrean gains. In 1987 the Derg dissolved the junta and set up the People's Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/workers-party-of-ethiopia">Workers' Party of Ethiopia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The 1983–1985 famine and economic decline increased support for the rebels, and in 1984 the Eritrean rebels regained the initiative. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> began ending its support in the late 1980s. On 28 May 1991 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Democratic Front</a>, a coalition of left-wing ethnic rebel groups, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, and Mengistu fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. A Transitional Government led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigray-peoples-liberation-front">Tigray People's Liberation Front</a> replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>. About 1 million of the dead were related to famine and the remainder to combat and other violence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ethiopian Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
